      "portalId": "1752b6cd-4139-4fa6-bc16-3b328562f94f",
      "title": "Lesson 57",
      "overview": "In this lesson, we delve into the foundational usooli maxim \"ما لا يتم الواجب إلا به فهو واجب\" — \"Whatever a wajib cannot be completed without becomes wajib itself\" — and explore its four major interpretations among scholars. The dominant view holds that whether the prerequisite is a sabab (cause) or shart (condition), it becomes wajib by necessity, as neglecting it would lead to takleef bil muhaal (commanding the impossible). Objections such as the claim that Allah’s command to pray doesn’t include wudhu, or that one must consciously intend the prerequisite, are answered by clarifying that the wajib here is not due to an explicit command but due to istilzam—logical entailment—meaning the command of salah inherently necessitates its prerequisites without needing a separate explicit directive. Practical implications are illustrated through scenarios like mistaken divorce declarations and marital confusion, highlighting how avoidance of haram becomes obligatory when certainty is unattainable.",
